8,4,8,8],[4,4,4,1,4,4,4,2,4,4,4,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,4,8,8],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,4,8,8],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,4,8,8],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,8,3,8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,4,8,8],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,9,4,4,4,8,4,4],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,9,4,4,4,8,4,4],[4,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,9,4,4,4,8,4,4],[4,4,4,1,4,4,4,2,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,4,9,4,4,4,8,4,4]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Row 0 is a legend strip. It is mostly background, with a few single coloured \"seed\" cells. Each seed has a colour `c` at some column `x`. Below the strip are large solid rectangles of various colours, sitting on the background.\n\n**What changes.** All the changed cells in the fact sheet lie in columns that hold a seed: columns 5 and 9 in pair 1, and columns 2, 5, 9, 20 and 28 in pair 2. Each seed drops a vertical line from row 1 to the bottom of the grid. Along that line, each cell is rewritten as follows:\n\n- If the cell is **not** colour `c` (background or a rectangle of another colour), it becomes `c`.\n- If the cell is **already** colour `c` (it lies inside a rectangle of the seed's own colour), it becomes **background**. The line \"cuts\" a gap through its own colour.\n\n**Checking this against the demonstrations:**\n\n- **Pair 1.**\n  - Seed 2 at column 5: background rows 1–5 and 12–19 turn 2, while rows 6–11 inside the 2-rectangle turn 8 (the background).\n  - Seed 4 at column 9: every cell turns 4. That includes the 2-rectangle cells, matching the fact sheet's \"2->4 x6\".\n- **Pair 2.**\n  - Seed 8 at column 2: the 1-block (rows 1–9) turns 8, and the 8-block (rows 19–27) turns 7.\n  - Seed 4 at column 20: the 3-block turns 4, and the 4-block (rows 19–26) turns 7.\n  - Seed 2 at column 9: rows 13–18 inside the 2-block turn 7, and the other cells turn 2.\n  - Every transition listed in the fact sheet fits: 7→c, other colour→c, and c→7.\n\n**Applying it to the test.**\n- The background is 4. The seeds in row 0 are 1@3, 2@7, 3@12, 3@18, 9@23 and 8@27.\n- Column 3 of colour 1 crosses the large 1-block (rows 2–15), so those cells become 4. The rows outside that block turn 1. Column 3 also crosses the lower 1-block (rows 23–28), and those cells become 4 too.\n- Column 7 of colour 2 clears a gap through the 2-block (rows 13–20). Elsewhere it paints 2, including over the 1-blocks.\n- Columns 12 and 18 of colour 3: column 18 passes through the 3-block (rows 3–7), so it is cut to 4 there. Column 12 does not cross the 3-block, so it only paints 3 over the other colours.\n- Column 23 of colour 9 cuts through the 9-block (rows 10–15).\n- Column 27 of colour 8 cuts through the 8-block (rows 20–25).\n\n**Order of operations.** The seed columns are distinct, so the lines never overlap. Each seed column can therefore be processed independently using the original input colours. I emit the canvas once after each line is drawn.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g[1:]) if g.shape[0] > 1 else background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for x in range(W):\n        c = g[0, x]\n        if c == bg:\n            continue\n        for y in range(1, H):\n            out[y, x] = bg if g[y, x] == c else c\n        emit(out, f\"Seed colour {c} at column {x} drops a line to the bottom: \"\n                  f\"cells of colour {c} become background {bg}, all others become {c}.\")\n    emit(out, \"All seed lines drawn.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Row 0 is a legend strip."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f01531d9460ffb9e348342248f8af9f975cecfdadcaa9290d3054d7a49bbc67c","task_sha256":"74c0e2ebbc80ee059e820a32094c3ce40cb3a1319fbba22355c0e5a28c3ddb26","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
